00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.530
STEFANO PASQUALI: So what
I'm going to present today

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.740
is my personal journey
on liquidity research

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:19.550
that started almost 10 years ago
and, in particular, the journey

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:22.040
in BlackRock, where
I started to run

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.070
this project three years ago.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:33.410
And the topic of liquidity is a
typical financial problem that

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.970
is very difficult to solve.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.430
That's why the papers and
research on liquidity,

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.120
they arrive pretty
late in the industry

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.110
and in the standard methodology.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:45.070
And because of sparse
data, because of--

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.220
or sparse, noisy data
or multidimensionality.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.970
So this is a typical
type of problem where

00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:51.920
machine learning can help, OK?

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.260
So what I'm going to
show to you is, first

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.790
of all, what is the AI for us.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:05.060
So what I think is
the right uses for us

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:08.630
in finance in particular for
my specific project for machine

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.090
learning, AI.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:14.040
Why and when in finance,
in particular in liquidity,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.940
the problem of hunting
for performance,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.430
but be aware of our black boxes.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.550
And I will present some model.

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.890
I will review the model
we already implemented

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:26.720
and give you a tour
of the lab models

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.590
that we are testing in the lab.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:34.040
So a little bit of names.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:39.100
So for me, machine learning is
making sense of a large data

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.420
set, recognizing complex
patterns in the data,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.187
and is being able to
account for non-linearity

00:01:48.187 --> 00:01:50.270
and getting rid of other
mathematical assumptions,

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.840
like assuming linearity
of some behave

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.718
or some of the features
or normality, all

00:01:55.718 --> 00:01:56.510
this kind of stuff.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.510
The typical mathematical
assumptions,

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:02.050
they were done in what I
call Quant Finance 1.0.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.350
To me, machine learning--

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.600
I prefer to use the word
"machine learning" than "AI."

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.580
So far, my machine
is not intelligent.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.370
It's just a machine that
can crunch out the data

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.980
and extracting
patterns in that data.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.710
And some of these factor
behaviors are good for me

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.400
as features in the job
of forecasting liquidity

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:24.410
in our security level, portfolio
level, redemption at risk,

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.540
or whatever we do.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.600
There are some cool names here.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.370
These are the ones dear to me,
because I started many years

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:38.300
ago to approach to leverage
and allow cluster analysis

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.970
in these types of problems.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.160
Pattern recognition is
what I say, regression,

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.000
because now everybody is
ashamed to say "regression."

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.010
No, we still use regression.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:53.310
And I'm not ashamed
that last quarter we

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.608
allowed free model
reproduction of two

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.150
based on machine
learning approaches.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.720
And one was linear regression
with two variables.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.100
Does its job.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:04.640
No shame in doing that.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.950
So I am a guy looking
for innovation.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:11.305
I am dreaming to be able to
build for liquidity default

00:03:11.305 --> 00:03:13.983
to serious deep learning model.

00:03:13.983 --> 00:03:15.400
Interpretability
is not a problem.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.567
I'm going to mention to you.

00:03:16.567 --> 00:03:18.530
But if you have something
that does its job

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:20.210
and is a simple,
interpretable model,

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.540
two variables, inner
regression, take it.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:27.510
Now, machine learning
is not that one.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.520
So it's not a robot.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:29.690
In particular in finance.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:30.950
Think about finance.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.500
We have a fiduciary
role to our clients.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.560
So I'm building a model,
providing the analytics

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.080
to a portfolio
manager or a trader.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.810
And this guy is making
business decisions

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.060
with other people's money.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:46.970
So we cannot use black
boxes, things that we cannot

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:47.580
understand.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:52.070
So for me, machine learning
is a [INAUDIBLE] scenario.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.568
Maybe one day, but not now.

00:03:53.568 --> 00:03:55.360
And there will be enough
of all on that day

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.600
when it's going to happen.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:01.340
And when you recognize
pattern in financial data,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.340
you can recognize that pattern.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:07.370
So there is a correlation
between the interest rates

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.810
and height of the Fed chairs.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.770
So you know better than me
that if you plug these features

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:16.310
in a machine, whatever
is your regression,

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:18.360
these will be
significant features.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.010
So now you pretend to know the
next height or the Fed chair,

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:25.020
you are claiming to
forecast interest rates.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.500
So you must be careful
about this type of pattern

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.750
that the machine will include.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.610
In particular, a machine in
the case of deep learning,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.090
we'll use this pattern
without telling it to you.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:41.272
Just set up the stage
right in the middle

00:04:41.272 --> 00:04:43.230
between myth and reality
of application machine

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:45.550
learning in finance.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.580
Explainable AI, XAI.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.780
People love acronyms.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.140
This is a new acronym that--

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.530
I mean, I started to see these
acronyms a few years ago.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:03.360
And we must enable
the user to understand

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.070
what the algorithms do.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:07.700
So basically, this is a
beautiful chart [INAUDIBLE]

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.990
by a presentation done by DARPA.

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.800
But it's beautiful to
represent the function

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:18.360
between explainability and
the learning performance.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:23.550
So clearly, we are now moving--

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:26.580
in particular in our
case in this area,

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.560
the dream is to go
to deep learning.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.080
But we still have
something to do

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.372
before we get to deep learning.

00:05:32.372 --> 00:05:35.790
But for sure, we are able
to prove that traditional

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:37.170
or machine learning
methodology--

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:39.810
if we call random forest
machine learning and they do--

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.960
can add important
improvements on performance,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.530
particularly now that we have a
lot of data to train the model.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:54.990
So this picture is our
guideline for our daily job

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.710
when we do in the lab, we
start to push on innovation.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:02.507
So our journey in machine
learning applied to liquidity.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.900
And, again, a few years
ago when I started to

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.398
work on liquidity--

00:06:12.398 --> 00:06:14.190
people in finance here
know better than me,

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.685
but there were a few people
working on fixed income,

00:06:17.685 --> 00:06:19.500
in particular, 10 years ago.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.570
There was no data.

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:27.390
Trades was a system where
trades data were reported

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.190
for US corporate bonds.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:30.040
Highly noisy.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.380
No competitor sparring.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.680
We kept-- et cetera.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:38.550
And there was nothing in Europe,
almost nothing in Europe.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.130
Nothing in emerging market.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.350
Equity, you had a lot of
data, but highly noisy.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:47.070
And there wasn't a
practice about model

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.290
to forecast the
liquidity of a security.

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:54.930
So you have a situation where
we have a lot of data, highly

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:57.185
noisy, so we need to start
seeing that from this

00:06:57.185 --> 00:06:58.990
and assemble the noisy data.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:03.990
Or you have entire market, where
we have a decent amount of data

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.480
only on a small
part of the market.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.530
And it come out with a
forecast for the rest

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.130
of the security in the market.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.650
So the principle here--

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.990
every trader, every
portfolio manager

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:21.120
will do exactly taking
a bond, for example,

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.540
putting the bond
in excess, start

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.020
to put in excess some
features downloaded

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.580
by information provider.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.930
Then, based on
experience, we start

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:33.030
to define the list of similar
bonds based on some feature--

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:36.330
rating, coupon, amount of
standing, et cetera, and trying

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.213
to pick up--

00:07:39.213 --> 00:07:41.630
have a sense of price and the
liquidity of the target bond

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.282
and start to evaluate,
look in similar bonds.

00:07:44.282 --> 00:07:46.130
The beautiful thing
about machine learning--

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:48.088
cluster analysis, random
forest that basically,

00:07:48.088 --> 00:07:49.620
more or less, does that thing.

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.640
And you can scale and
automatize that thing.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.800
So that one, I think,
was the first adventure.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:08:00.640
Now, what we do here?

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.680
This is the description of
the entire overall project.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.625
So because of this problem
of estimating liquidity,

00:08:06.625 --> 00:08:08.550
liquidity arrive very late.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.640
And liquidity arrive-- if
you look at best practice

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.067
for a credit model,
default probability model,

00:08:14.067 --> 00:08:15.900
we can agree or not
agree with those models,

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:19.830
but there are plenty of
models from the '80s, deep

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.460
mathematical models,
very complex models.

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.570
In liquidity, there
is almost nothing--

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.390
a couple of papers almost
playing with the same three

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:29.490
concepts back and forth.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.010
Is that because no
data, et cetera?

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:35.240
And so people was keeping
liquidity a little bit

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.950
outside the risk
managing and process.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.548
I do market risk,
I do credit risk,

00:08:38.548 --> 00:08:40.590
I do portfolio optimization,
good tracking error,

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.230
and then on the side,
I try to figure it out

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.860
to have a sense what is the
liquidity in my portfolios.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:51.620
Now regulators gave
me a job, because they

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:55.320
created 10 years ago
on the sell side first.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:56.910
Then on the buy
side, they started

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:01.140
to push for people to
adopt liquidity metrics

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.650
to control liquidity risk.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:06.430
In particular, BlackRock decided
to have a more broader vision,

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:10.600
so really embedded liquidity
in the risk management process.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:13.170
And many years ago
when I was trying

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:15.300
to convince my former
company to invest

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:18.600
in this type of research,
I was mentioning that--

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:20.310
you should think
about value at risk.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:25.720
Why is it used by everybody
since 30 years, maybe 25 years?

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.460
It's not necessarily the
right risk management,

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.050
but it creates a
baseline out of which

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.790
compliance people,
portfolio manager,

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:36.540
risk manager tool
based on some numbers.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:40.020
I want to address how good
or bad is value at risk.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.580
But value at risk
creates this link.

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:46.590
And if you think,
embedded in value at risk,

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:48.965
there is one crazy assumption--

00:09:48.965 --> 00:09:51.090
that you have historical
[INAUDIBLE] of the price--

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.350
how to reach and generate
your distribution.

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:57.270
So every single position
and the price 20 days

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:02.040
ago, you could have sold
that day your entire position

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.710
immediately in the
market at that price.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:07.300
That's simply not true.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:10.770
Because when you want to
unwind the position, then

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:14.550
you need to decide
the venue, decide how

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.520
you want to sell this position.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.753
If your position is big,
you're going to have a cost.

00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:22.170
So a lot of people
start to become

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.620
aware of the concept about
liquidity-adjusted value

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:26.340
at risk.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:31.110
So this was a way to plug
liquidity from the beginning

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:32.550
into data management process.

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:37.260
So what we have to do was to
do a gigantic infrastructure.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.540
Unfortunately for my
job, every company

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.060
I went, I was hired
to build models.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.570
I started to build models
after 18 months, 24 a month.

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.860
The first three years
was building databases,

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:53.735
cleaning the data, going around
the company hunting for data.

00:10:53.735 --> 00:10:55.860
If the data weren't there,
I convinced the big guys

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.867
to have funding for the data.

00:10:57.867 --> 00:10:58.950
Pulling the data together.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:01.990
So it was necessary to
create this environment.

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:03.890
That's exactly what we did here.

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.660
So for liquidity,
there was a necessity

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.280
to unify the data
we were having,

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:13.410
internal data from
BlackRock, external vendor,

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:16.570
pull [INAUDIBLE]
external information.

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:19.770
Crunching this with a
high-performance computing--

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:22.170
in this case, we
used a lot of GPUs.

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:23.782
We created our
research environment

00:11:23.782 --> 00:11:25.740
and were necessarily
based on machine learning.

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:31.600
We called this, actually,
our Liquidity Vault.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.740
We have all this magic data
to build the liquidity models.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:36.720
Then we go here.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.870
And the first job, we want
to build transaction cost.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:42.000
We want to have simulated that.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.130
Because of portfolio
optimizations, rebalancing,

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:47.460
or whatever, we wanted
to sell a specific volume

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:48.315
of this position.

00:11:48.315 --> 00:11:50.010
It can be a bond,
an equity, whatever.

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:53.460
We wanted to have an idea of how
much it was going to cost us.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:57.200
And if we wait more
time or less time,

00:11:57.200 --> 00:11:59.230
how the price is
going to change?

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.600
So we want to build this
liquidity transaction cost

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:02.790
surface.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:08.290
Then we wanted to aggregate the
portfolio level, the liquidity,

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:09.960
and this is tricky.

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:14.070
Because the concept of
correlation of liquidity,

00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:17.040
the typical way people, they
aggregate the portfolio level

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.150
is trickier.

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:21.313
And if you start to sell
a bond in a portfolio

00:12:21.313 --> 00:12:23.730
and you add bond of same issue
in the same portfolio, when

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.670
you go to sell the second bond,
maybe the liquidity profile

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:31.990
of this bond has changed because
of the first sale you did.

00:12:31.990 --> 00:12:34.890
So here, I think
there is a better way

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:37.740
to approach this problem
I'm going to mention to you.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.360
Then the other
side of liquidity.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.120
And that, for us, as
managers, is very important.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.810
Assuming that my portfolios
are liquid or in control

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.810
of liquidity, I can't receive
redemption for my clients

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:49.860
on the fund.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.290
So I need to be able
to unwind the position

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:55.200
at a reasonable cost, because
I'm controlling liquidity risk,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.830
to give cash back to the people
that are redeeming the fund

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:01.500
and not to hurt the
people still in the fund.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:07.800
So I discovered that
the redemption at risk

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:09.760
is one of the most
difficult problems

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.500
ever, because thinking
of being able to forecast

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:15.180
that, in this particular
fund, I would have

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.395
these outflows next
week or next month

00:13:17.395 --> 00:13:19.020
is a very difficult
problem, because it

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:20.250
depends on market conditions.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.590
Specifically, it does syncretic
information on the fund.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:28.680
Behavior of people depends
from if the fund is widely

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:31.898
used for pension fund
for other company.

00:13:31.898 --> 00:13:34.190
If there is a moment where
a lot of people will retire,

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:36.357
we're going to have structured
outflows in the fund.

00:13:36.357 --> 00:13:42.340
So there are so many things
to account in the fund.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:45.630
So basically, here
is a case where

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:49.450
we try to clean the message
with a linear regression,

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:51.360
a gradient-boosting
machine less regression.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:54.750
But the only way where we
found some decent performance

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:55.950
was using neural networks.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.640
So the liquidity analytics
as built at the beginning,

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:05.660
we plugged this into a
portfolio optimization,

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:10.590
and our dream is to arrive to
this concept, where we have--

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:11.670
this is the portfolio.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:14.380
Actually, it's a chart,
but it's a real simulation

00:14:14.380 --> 00:14:19.680
of a real fund, where
you have cash, the cost,

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.520
and you want to allow
the tracking error--

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.530
you want to set pro-rata
or allow the portfolio

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:28.248
to optimize the
liquidity profile.

00:14:28.248 --> 00:14:30.040
And you want to basically
control the risk.

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:33.520
So basically, try to have the
highest possible cash raise

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:34.770
with the lowest possible cost.

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.917
So you want to try to be
in this area of the curve.

00:14:37.917 --> 00:14:39.750
And then when you have
this, you can finally

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:40.625
make everybody happy.

00:14:40.625 --> 00:14:44.520
You serve, with one centralized
engine, portfolio construction,

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:47.110
training support,
liquidity-adjusted risk, what

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:51.420
I mentioned before, and
regulatory reporting.

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:56.220
For some things, we are in
a good stage of the project

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.120
to serve these guys.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:15:01.140
We are still working for
the coverage in order

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:05.460
to implement-- to plug into
this optimizer in our platform.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.010
But this will give you a
sense of the broader project

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.975
that we are doing on liquidity.

00:15:12.950 --> 00:15:16.380
To honor my guest-- so the
Google people that kindly

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:17.250
