00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:06.590
Now, here, a hand drawing
to see how typical

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:09.610
the problem we need to solve.

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:10.570
Bid and ask spread.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:11.610
Straight line.

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:12.970
Useless.

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:16.090
Because that, to you, that you
have the same cost independent

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:18.460
of how much you want to sell.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:22.690
The theory is that every,
every, transaction cost

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:26.880
model in every paper, more
or less, is using a sublinear

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:29.110
approach, the typical
square root function.

00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:31.970
Or you can spend hours to
recalibrate the exponent

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:35.530
as 0.6, 0.4 with this approach.

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:40.120
Those models are very poor,
because the blue points

00:18:40.120 --> 00:18:42.410
are more or less how
the data will look.

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:43.520
Very noisy.

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:47.553
But actually, the cost goes down
when [INAUDIBLE] more volume.

00:18:47.553 --> 00:18:49.720
Then it's almost flat around
the bid and ask spread.

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.190
Then we start to spike up.

00:18:51.190 --> 00:18:52.857
And when it spikes
up, typically, you

00:18:52.857 --> 00:18:54.190
don't have a lot of observation.

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:56.670
I'm talking about
three, four observations

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:58.330
per bond in a quarter.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:01.150
So how can you fit the
model in that area?

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:03.830
So the real curve
is the green one.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:06.430
The theory says,
try to calibrate

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:08.290
a model using the red one.

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.515
And now we think that
neural network approach,

00:19:11.515 --> 00:19:14.170
deep learning, we can
help a lot in the future

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:19.150
to actually go non-parametric,
and going to fit much better,

00:19:19.150 --> 00:19:22.060
and forecast much
better this curve.

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:24.170
There is typically
the 3D problem.

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.290
So I want to have a
model that, together,

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:31.330
can account for the slippage of
the curve in functional time.

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:35.020
So now if I have a cost
of 10 basis points,

00:19:35.020 --> 00:19:37.600
starting 10 million,
if I wait more time,

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.520
I will be able to
lower the cost.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:43.540
So that's definitely a problem
people are trying to solve.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:47.798
And the similarity constant--
this is very important,

00:19:47.798 --> 00:19:49.840
because it's something
that you must account when

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.060
you build liquidity models.

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:53.020
Bond 1, Bond 2.

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:55.030
Assuming that this
is a good curve.

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:56.920
Stay with me now.

00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:00.310
I'm selling something in Bond 1.

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:02.860
Eventually, I'm going to
shift the curve of Bond 2.

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:05.350
So when I'm going to
go to sell the Bond 2,

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.990
I have a different
transaction cost curve.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:11.680
And this [INAUDIBLE]
can be attacked,

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.630
this problem, using
security similarity,

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:17.398
making a linkage
between securities.

00:20:17.398 --> 00:20:20.110
Google has the Knowledge
Graph, a similar concept

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:22.810
about this-- making a
link between entities--

00:20:22.810 --> 00:20:27.210
in this case, security
in general or bonds.

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:30.730
Now, the data.

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:33.720
First of all, let me go back.

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:39.210
Whatever equation curve you
use, you have observed cost

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:44.980
and you are aiming to train
a model based on this cost.

00:20:44.980 --> 00:20:47.525
People coming from
other sectors-- that's

00:20:47.525 --> 00:20:49.400
why I asked the question
before-- they think,

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:53.770
OK, in historical series of
something that I observe,

00:20:53.770 --> 00:20:56.710
that's the Bible,
that's true, and now I

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:59.080
can train my model,
whatever model it is.

00:20:59.080 --> 00:21:02.830
In finance, I'm sorry, the
situation is not like this.

00:21:02.830 --> 00:21:06.730
To provoke you to understand
this concept, here I did--

00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:10.410
this is my transaction
cost cooling function

00:21:10.410 --> 00:21:13.820
of volume traded within a
spread [INAUDIBLE] volume

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:16.050
and other features.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:18.460
The independent
variable is this,

00:21:18.460 --> 00:21:20.350
called Implementation Shortfall.

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:22.990
It's the differential
between a trade

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:25.880
that I did and
dimensional price.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:27.860
So the market was at this level.

00:21:27.860 --> 00:21:29.360
I trade at this
level or this level?

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:32.530
This definition is the cost
that I got in the moment.

00:21:32.530 --> 00:21:35.190
I looked at the cost,
I trained the model,

00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:38.350
and tried to forecast in
the future of this cost.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.160
Here is a very liquid bond.

00:21:40.160 --> 00:21:40.990
Very easy.

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:44.590
It's a telecommunication bond.

00:21:44.590 --> 00:21:47.410
Between 3%, 4% coupon.

00:21:47.410 --> 00:21:49.150
Maturity, 2022.

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:52.400
And to calculate this
implementation shortfall,

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:56.140
our trades [INAUDIBLE]
3D information provided.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:59.500
Everybody claiming, I
give you the right price.

00:21:59.500 --> 00:22:01.625
Look the three curves.

00:22:01.625 --> 00:22:03.000
So now you're
trading at whatever

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.617
it is-- a linear regression
or a neural network.

00:22:06.617 --> 00:22:08.200
When you are here,
you are in trouble,

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.876
because you don't know
what is the truth.

00:22:10.876 --> 00:22:14.980
So I told to my guys, I'm
sorry, guys, but 60% of your job

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:17.090
is [INAUDIBLE]
and cleaning data.

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:20.230
This is something that
we have to do internally.

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:23.780
That's why the application of
machine learning in finance,

00:22:23.780 --> 00:22:27.377
there are many reasons,
but the fact that we

00:22:27.377 --> 00:22:29.960
are dealing with real money, but
as well this type of problem.

00:22:29.960 --> 00:22:33.183
Data to train the models
are often not available

00:22:33.183 --> 00:22:34.850
or, now that they're
becoming available,

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:39.320
can be very noisy,
very inconsistent.

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:40.450
Now, what do we do here?

00:22:43.118 --> 00:22:44.660
This was the
implementation shortfall

00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:46.440
that I mentioned before.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:49.850
So we have our benchmark price.

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:50.860
We execute a trade.

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:54.550
The differential is
our observed cost

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:58.610
out of which we train our model,
whatever methodology we use.

00:22:58.610 --> 00:23:03.980
And our model is defined
on many important features.

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:06.260
Fixed cost-- typically
the bid and ask spread.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:09.306
The volume forecast.

00:23:09.306 --> 00:23:12.680
I know that this bond has
high level tradable volume.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:14.825
Whatever I want to sell,
I have a lower cost.

00:23:14.825 --> 00:23:18.120
A bond with lower tradable
volume in the market,

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:19.750
if I grow closer
to this level, I'm

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:21.410
going to have a
spike with the cost.

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:23.030
And other features.

00:23:23.030 --> 00:23:27.710
And I show this diagram,
because for a moment,

00:23:27.710 --> 00:23:29.660
instead of going wild
and deep learning,

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:33.080
all this kind of stuff, we did
the entire transaction cost,

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:37.220
we only, for one component
of the transaction cost,

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:38.990
we used machine learning.

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:41.570
And now we are attacking
the other part.

00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:42.980
And here we use random forest.

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:45.442
We have an implementation in
the lab with neural network

00:23:45.442 --> 00:23:46.400
with the same problem--

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:47.630
interpretability.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:50.732
And this is very important,
because this model now

00:23:50.732 --> 00:23:51.440
is in production.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:53.600
It works very well.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:58.910
So far our customer, the
traders are very happy.

00:23:58.910 --> 00:24:01.478
The problem that I think about--

00:24:01.478 --> 00:24:03.270
let's assume that I
have the perfect model.

00:24:03.270 --> 00:24:06.370
I don't, by the way, but I
have a decent performance.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:08.900
Tomorrow I forecast
for a specific bond

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:11.540
a big drop in
tradable volume, so

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:14.750
as a signal, very severe
warning for our traders.

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:17.135
They need to do something
about that or not.

00:24:17.135 --> 00:24:18.780
Kind of quarantine the bond.

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:21.280
If you have to unwind some
position, not that one,

00:24:21.280 --> 00:24:23.660
or start to sell.

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:27.620
To implement a strategy to
hedge half from this liquidity

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:29.060
risk on this bond.

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:32.300
If I say there is a drop of
50% of the tradable volume,

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:33.630
it's a big thing.

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:35.540
Next thing the guy
is going to ask me--

00:24:35.540 --> 00:24:36.680
why?

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:39.170
And I cannot tell to him, I
have a beautiful deep-learning

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:39.890
model.

00:24:39.890 --> 00:24:41.690
I have it tested, I'm cool.

00:24:41.690 --> 00:24:43.760
My guys, as smart
computer-sense people,

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:46.770
take it, because this
guy is making decisions,

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:49.980
trading real people's money.

00:24:49.980 --> 00:24:52.310
That's why, in neural
network, we can pick up

00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:54.060
some sort of interpretability.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:56.880
Sorry, in a random
forest, we can pick up

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.130
some sort of interpretability.

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:00.620
Now I can say to the guy,
I have an example here.

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.620
Why the model is moving?

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:04.790
It's forecasting
something different.

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:08.810
So only on the
volume forecast where

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:12.840
is this framework, where
you're applying neural network.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.833
And we basically--
now I don't want

00:25:15.833 --> 00:25:17.000
to go into too much details.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.042
I'm happy to provide to
you offline some details.

00:25:19.042 --> 00:25:21.580
But here, what we
do here, we trade

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:25.370
through random forest,
one on empirical data,

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:27.950
using empirical
volume data; and then

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:31.830
other one based on features in
absence of transactional data.

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.840
So we are able to
leverage to have

00:25:34.840 --> 00:25:38.450
a guess of the tradeable
volume for a bond that

00:25:38.450 --> 00:25:39.380
never shows volume.

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:41.280
A bond that never shows
volume, for example,

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:42.710
doesn't mean that
it's in liquid.

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:44.750
It's a bond that
[INAUDIBLE] trade,

00:25:44.750 --> 00:25:46.700
but you can go to
trade this bond easily,

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:50.660
or it's a signal that
this bond is untradeable.

00:25:50.660 --> 00:25:55.490
And then we have some concept
of a probability of trade.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:25:58.920
So we separated
the model in trying

00:25:58.920 --> 00:26:03.260
to define the probability,
how is the probability

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:05.510
that this bond's going to
trade and then when it's

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:09.080
going to trade, which
is the expected value.

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:12.535
And this model so far
is working pretty well.

00:26:12.535 --> 00:26:14.660
And it's the one that has
a neural network parallel

00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:16.760
approach on this.

00:26:16.760 --> 00:26:19.520
Transaction cost overall.

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:23.750
I'll tell you this sad story
of the progress of this model.

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:26.070
It's very important.

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:29.900
So we have an initial model,
a linear regression model

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:31.520
based on the intraday data.

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:35.690
The X means very poor
[INAUDIBLE] performance.

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:38.360
I can tell you, only between
us, that the [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:40.880
performance of
this model was 1%.

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.910
So pretty low.

00:26:42.910 --> 00:26:46.970
The reason why I'm putting
these dots and not the numbers--

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:49.135
because some people
in my company,

00:26:49.135 --> 00:26:50.690
they warned me,
listen, you're going

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:52.600
in front of Google people.

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:55.705
And there will be a lot
of non-financial people.

00:26:55.705 --> 00:26:57.830
And then you're going to
brag about the performance

00:26:57.830 --> 00:27:01.690
on the last line that, in
finance, is a very big number.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:03.950
I would say, in finance,
it's a ridiculous number.

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:06.270
Well, not ridiculous,
but a low number.

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:08.490
So just to avoid the
credibility issues with you,

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:10.090
I hid the number.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:11.690
So I've been discovering, no?

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:14.210
So the fourth one was very bad.

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:18.080
When I went to intraday
data, I did some work

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:20.650
on defining better the
implementation shortfall

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:21.650
that I mentioned before.

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:24.530
The model was OK.

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:27.700
Then we started to do
partitioning on the universe.

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:29.750
So we started to
separate buy and sell.

00:27:29.750 --> 00:27:31.610
Then we started to separate--

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:34.370
if you think we started to do
a concept of manual cluster.

00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:38.160
We are bucketing the universe in
more and more generalist groups

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:40.700
and fitting the model
within each group.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:44.720
And if you think, in one
shot, a random forest will do.

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:49.130
And now we are working a lot
on a generalized additive

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:51.860
model, non-parametric
model like random forest.

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:56.000
And we are, as well, in the
lab, testing neural network.

00:27:56.000 --> 00:27:59.270
This is neural network for
the entire transaction cost.

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:01.790
So not only one component.

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:04.850
We expected that if we train the
model of a big amount of data,

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:06.290
the performance
will be very high.

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:07.280
I don't know yet.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:10.670
Next event, you will
know how the model works.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:13.160
So far, we've rolled
out in production

00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:16.850
this model, based on
its aggressive approach,

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:18.840
based on the partition
of the universe.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:20.960
Now we are generalizing
this with cluster analysis

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:21.900
and random forest.

00:28:21.900 --> 00:28:22.970
That's our next step.

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:24.803
But so far, we've rolled
out, in production,

00:28:24.803 --> 00:28:28.370
this model that is using the
ADV based on random forest.

00:28:28.370 --> 00:28:32.310
And so far, the model
performs pretty well.

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:36.080
To give you some story of the
evolution of our experiment.

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:40.290
This is an example
of November 2000--

00:28:40.290 --> 00:28:44.000
OK, my company was
very lucky to have,

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:48.650
in the last four years, any
serious liquidity stress.

00:28:48.650 --> 00:28:51.770
For me, it was a damnation,
because I have nothing

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:55.592
to train the data
on liquidity stress.

00:28:55.592 --> 00:28:57.800
So I say this against the
interest of my company now.

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:00.310
I needed some bad moment,
so I can verify my model.

00:29:00.310 --> 00:29:04.730
Otherwise, everything's
good, my model is good.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:06.840
Something like this
happened in November.

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:08.340
There was some
stress in the market,

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.400
particularly in
corporate bond market.

00:29:10.400 --> 00:29:11.960
And we are very
happy to see that,

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:14.510
from beginning of November
up to Thanksgiving,

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:18.573
where the stress was
very evident to manifest,

00:29:18.573 --> 00:29:19.490
there was a situation.

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:20.532
That's why we have a job.

00:29:20.532 --> 00:29:23.895
Situation where portfolio
manager, they were ordering--

00:29:23.895 --> 00:29:26.130
OK, I need to unwind
this position.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.593
That's fine, because bid and
ask spread is not moving.

00:29:28.593 --> 00:29:30.260
And the traders say,
I'm sorry, but when

00:29:30.260 --> 00:29:33.930
I go to trade these guys,
it's more difficult.

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:35.570
I have a higher cost.

00:29:35.570 --> 00:29:38.090
And there was this
connection between the two.

00:29:38.090 --> 00:29:41.130
The concept of liquidity
doesn't depend necessarily

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:42.860
of observability
within a spread.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:45.050
It can depend from
many other features.

00:29:45.050 --> 00:29:46.880
So the entire
model, we are able--

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:49.590
this is something that you
cannot do in neural network.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:51.560
So I was inviting
[INAUDIBLE] spotlight,

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:53.965
Google to find a solution.

00:29:53.965 --> 00:29:56.090
This is something that you
can do in random forest.

00:29:56.090 --> 00:29:59.100
This is the evolution for
a group of three bonds.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:02.570
The issuer, average behavior
of the transaction cost

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:03.770
across November.

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:08.180
And the transaction cost went up
a lot in terms of basis points.

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:10.913
So what we could
unwind here were

00:30:10.913 --> 00:30:13.580
very different to what you could
unwind at the end of the month.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:15.710
And you see that
duration times spread

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:17.390
was backing up as expected.

00:30:17.390 --> 00:30:19.000
Within a spread,
it's almost flat.

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:22.270
Bid and ask spread in that
month actually went down,

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:24.670
even counter-intuitively.

00:30:24.670 --> 00:30:27.650
It was 0.2 basis
points, so nothing.

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:29.700
But ADV was dropping.

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:33.500
And interestingly, the number
of dealer runs was dropping.

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:35.000
So once the feature
was used, it's

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:37.790
just simply count how many
dealer runs are contributing

00:30:37.790 --> 00:30:38.890
to this bond.

00:30:38.890 --> 00:30:40.253
It's a measure of interest.

00:30:40.253 --> 00:30:42.170
I know that there are
some Google people there

00:30:42.170 --> 00:30:44.610
that are going to have a lot
of ideas about the [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:44.610 --> 00:30:45.443
and stuff like this.

00:30:45.443 --> 00:30:48.500
But for the moment, for us,
counting how many dealer runs

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:51.317
are contributing a
bond is an indication

00:30:51.317 --> 00:30:53.900
of interest around this bond and
making bids around this bond.

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:55.710
Now, this number drops.

00:30:55.710 --> 00:30:56.800
For us, it's a warning.

00:30:56.800 --> 00:30:58.430
The model reacted to this.

00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:00.440
So bid and ask spread
that is technically

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:03.770
the main driver of transaction
cost wasn't moving,

00:31:03.770 --> 00:31:06.290
but the other features, less
important, a lot of them,

00:31:06.290 --> 00:31:07.130
they were moving.

00:31:07.130 --> 00:31:10.760
And finally, the transaction
cost was doing a good job.

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:11.780
I did my test.

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:13.080
I claimed the model was done.

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:15.170
Now I don't want any more
stress in the market,

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:17.470
because it was a very
painful month for me.

00:31:17.470 --> 00:31:18.520
Anyway.

00:31:18.520 --> 00:31:20.900
But this is exactly the reason
why we are doing this job.

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:24.740
So now we can provide a signal
[INAUDIBLE] to the traders,

00:31:24.740 --> 00:31:29.060
and we can attribute this
to the input features.

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.700
Idea for next step.

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:34.560
Very quick, I mentioned to
you that we have, in the lab,

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:37.173
generalized additive
models that we tried.

00:31:37.173 --> 00:31:38.840
And for this different
group of markets,

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:44.090
we improved the performance of
the model of 6%, 22%, and 11%.

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:45.170
And that's very natural.

00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:48.850
It's a kind of fitting
sublinear model, different model

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:51.200
for every band of volumes.

00:31:51.200 --> 00:31:52.570
So it's the orange curve.

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.340
If you remember, it's exactly
similar to the empirical shape

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:57.320
of the data in the first
slide I showed to you.

00:31:57.320 --> 00:32:00.670
Going down, then up.

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:03.920
Then one thing, with
my guys, we start

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:08.330
soon in the lab is a leveraged
reinforcement learning

00:32:08.330 --> 00:32:10.700
on the transaction cost level.

00:32:10.700 --> 00:32:13.790
And this, we think,
is going to be

00:32:13.790 --> 00:32:16.610
performing very, very
well, technically,

00:32:16.610 --> 00:32:20.570
and particularly useful
for stress testing.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:23.173
I have nothing to say to you
now, because we need to start.

00:32:23.173 --> 00:32:24.590
We are configuring
the environment

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:25.820
to do this experiment.

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:28.940
But this is an evolution
of the transaction cost

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:30.040
we're going to do.

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:32.480
Similarity.

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:35.060
This is our-- and I've
been bragging about this

00:32:35.060 --> 00:32:36.990
and talking about
this for 10 years.

00:32:36.990 --> 00:32:40.050
So we have now
different methodologies

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.530
to be able to define
given the first bond.

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:46.520
In this case, I picked
up oil company bonds.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:49.860
Similar bond, this is
completely done by the machine.

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:51.560
So here, I can use
cluster analysis.

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:54.510
I can use
gradient-boosting tree.

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:56.830
You can use neural network.

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:58.780
And again, if you
use neural network,

00:32:58.780 --> 00:33:01.230
you're going to have an
interpretability problem.

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:04.590
But we are very
happy about this.

00:33:04.590 --> 00:33:09.105
And now you see that two
total bond are very similar.

00:33:09.105 --> 00:33:11.730
If you start to do some trading
activity on the first one, when

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:13.900
you go to make a trade
on the second one,

00:33:13.900 --> 00:33:17.920
you're going to have a
different liquidity profile.

00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:22.320
And so we are basically
defining this similarity base

00:33:22.320 --> 00:33:25.570
on how the securities are
highly connected or not.

00:33:25.570 --> 00:33:27.570
There is a model we are
implementing about this.

00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:33.480
So in the security similarity,
we have distance-based metrics.

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:35.520
That is done.

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:41.510
We are testing using
gradient-boosting trees.

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:44.340
And next, we're going to have
semi-supervised variational

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:46.500
auto-encoders and potentially
a knowledge graph.

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:49.500
I do believe that the
knowledge graph concept--

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:50.550
I don't know yet--

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:53.400
will be the answer in
terms of a methodology tool

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.790
to do this job.

00:33:55.790 --> 00:34:02.427
Again, to honor my guests, about
interpretability, in this case,

00:34:02.427 --> 00:34:04.260
interpretability becomes
much more important

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:05.970
than other cases,
because this will

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:07.920
be used widely,
technically, even

00:34:07.920 --> 00:34:09.490
for portfolio construction.

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.639
Many people were investigating,
but these two papers

00:34:14.639 --> 00:34:16.989
are done by Google people.

00:34:16.989 --> 00:34:20.820
I think it's DeepMind
people, I think.

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:23.219
So this is basically what
we are doing in the lab now.

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:28.800
The title here is
Redemption at Risk,

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:31.690
The Beginning of Our Artificial
Neural Network and Cloud

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:32.909
Adventure.

00:34:32.909 --> 00:34:36.949
Because if you think
about regressive models,

00:34:36.949 --> 00:34:40.139
random forests based
on now a lot of data,

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:43.530
but not petabytes
of data, you can

00:34:43.530 --> 00:34:48.560
work in an internal
infrastructure and databases.

00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:50.860
Random forest was a
difficult problem,

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:56.699
so immediately, we started to
end in some performance issues.

00:34:56.699 --> 00:35:00.120
Then we opened a personal
account on the Google Cloud.

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:00.630
Literally.

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:02.200
The first one was
a personal account.

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:03.700
We started to make
experiments, then

00:35:03.700 --> 00:35:06.240
we opened a corporate
account on that.

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:11.210
And basically, the story here--

00:35:11.210 --> 00:35:14.130
so we did this first experiment
without the data just

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:16.230
to understand how the
cloud was working.

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:20.370
Then we got approval to upload
some of our data in the cloud

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:22.320
officially, as a
company under contract.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:25.920
And we were able to do
this experiment, using

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:28.170
an embarrassing
amount of factors,

00:35:28.170 --> 00:35:31.980
because running the
redemption of the fund

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:34.320
has a crazy amount of factors.

00:35:34.320 --> 00:35:37.950
Using three ensembles
to filter the features

00:35:37.950 --> 00:35:39.850
and then fit in our networks.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:41.580
And the model we
are building is time

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:46.290
to forecast a CDF that we
can transform in a PDF.

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:48.900
It's telling to us the
probability of-- we

00:35:48.900 --> 00:35:50.970
are not forecasting the
size of the redemption.

00:35:50.970 --> 00:35:53.080
I would be a very rich
guy if I could do that.

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:55.110
We are trying to
forecast the probability

00:35:55.110 --> 00:35:57.720
of large flows, where
large is a flow bigger

00:35:57.720 --> 00:35:59.270
than a certain threshold.

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:02.430
So basically, you take your
slice on this 3D picture,

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:04.110
and then you use
that distribution

00:36:04.110 --> 00:36:06.330
to do your forecast,
a distribution

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:08.610
conditional to all
the input factors.

00:36:08.610 --> 00:36:11.190
This is the only way we
could have a somewhat decent

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:13.620
performance in this model.

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:16.890
Not crazy big, but a good
performance, something that you

00:36:16.890 --> 00:36:19.620
can use in the daily life, and
it was the only way to do that.

00:36:19.620 --> 00:36:22.650
Now, we are working
now in trying

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.770
to make sense of this model
for day-to-day users because

00:36:25.770 --> 00:36:28.710
of interpretability.

00:36:28.710 --> 00:36:33.240
Another case that
we are working on.

00:36:35.710 --> 00:36:37.460
I don't have to tell
the people in finance

00:36:37.460 --> 00:36:41.820
here that, in particular,
bonds trade over the counter.

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:45.150
This data is unstructured, and
there is not a quantitative way

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:47.950
to determine the accuracy
of the dealer quotes.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:52.020
Dealer quotes are the big source
of everybody, us included.

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:53.970
Every vendor I'm
using is because trade

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:57.210
are very rare, sparse.

00:36:57.210 --> 00:36:59.280
The information from
dealers are what people

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:01.240
use to price these bonds.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:05.850
And as you saw before, three
different providers 30 years

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:10.000
in the market, they have severe
disagreement of an easy bond.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:14.150
There is no way to determine
which one is better or not.

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:17.670
A lot, we have to rely
to subjective expertise

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:19.590
of the traders.

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:21.307
And one reason I
joined BlackRock--

00:37:21.307 --> 00:37:23.640
because I was in a company
where there was a big trading

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:27.870
desk because of the amount
of work the company has.

00:37:27.870 --> 00:37:29.950
So I was learning a lot.

00:37:29.950 --> 00:37:31.950
I thought to be an
expert in pricing bonds

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:35.480
and then, given the right
price to estimated liquidity,

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:36.690
I realized, no.

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:38.910
And I learned a lot
from our traders.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:42.100
So now how do we
transform this know-how

00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:44.680
without automatizing
[INAUDIBLE] know-how?

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:49.100
So you should remember the
benchmark price problem, no?

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:51.290
And then what we are
trying to do here

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:54.620
is basically collect and wait.

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:57.930
Different pricing
source and learning

00:37:57.930 --> 00:37:59.930
how to weight this from
feedback that we receive

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:04.190
from our traders
in a UI and trying

00:38:04.190 --> 00:38:06.800
to apply NLP to their comments.

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:08.720
So really, when they
disagree on the price,

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:11.100
they tend to ask,
in English, why

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:13.190
or put it in English
better than mine.

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:16.310
And then with NLP,
we try to extract

00:38:16.310 --> 00:38:18.263
features to adjust our model.

00:38:18.263 --> 00:38:19.930
We are on the beginning
of this journey,

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:21.950
but this is cool stuff.

00:38:21.950 --> 00:38:25.490
It was an idea enacted
[INAUDIBLE] two of my guys

00:38:25.490 --> 00:38:29.120
last year, and now we are
trying to make this a reality.

00:38:29.120 --> 00:38:32.120
This is just to introduce what
I think the same final answer

00:38:32.120 --> 00:38:35.790
that I mentioned yesterday
in the AI spotlight,

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:40.170
where I see the biggest
improvements, impacts

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:43.880
of machine learning on finance,
something that I'm not expert.

